uncertainty of statistics computed from the spectra, or of the parameters
of demographic models fitted with \code{dadi}, \code{moments} or \code{fastsimcoal2}
(see \code{\link[=write_sfs]{write_sfs()}} to write the replicates in their formats).
}
\details{
The loci are divided into non-overlapping blocks that never span more
than one chromosome, defined as in the \verb{jackknife_*} functions (e.g.
\code{\link[=jackknife_pop_tajimas_d]{jackknife_pop_tajimas_d()}}): blocks of \code{block_size} SNPs, base pairs or
centiMorgans (the latter requires the \code{genetic_dist} column of the loci
table). Blocks should be larger than the extent of linkage
disequilibrium, so that they are approximately independent. Each
replicate draws, with replacement, as many blocks as there are (as the
bootstrap of \code{dadi}, \code{Misc.bootstraps_from_dd_chunks()}). With
\code{block_size = 1} and \code{size_unit = "snp"}, single SNPs are resampled (as the
\code{--resample-vcf} option of \code{easySFS}), which ignores linkage.

A spectrum is a sum over loci, so the spectrum of a replicate is the sum
of the spectra of the blocks it drew (counting each block as many times as
it was drawn). The genotypes are therefore read only once, to compute the
spectrum of each block. Missing data are dealt with by projection, as in
\code{\link[=pop_sfs]{pop_sfs()}}, with the same projections (\code{n_proj}) for all replicates: if
\code{n_proj} is \code{NULL}, they are chosen from all the loci with
\code{\link[=pop_sfs_preview]{pop_sfs_preview()}}. The blocks are drawn with the random number
generator of R, so use \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} to make the replicates reproducible.
